 {"name": "No: he had predecessors", "who": "Scholars of the early Greek prose writers; Baruch Halpern (The First Historians, 1988)", "view": "Hecataeus and other Greek prose writers had already begun criticising myth and describing peoples, and Israel's historians in Samuel and Kings had written a connected, source-based account of their nation centuries earlier.", "evidence": "The fragment of Hecataeus calling the Greek stories 'many and absurd'; Kings' citations of 'the book of the chronicles of the kings of Israel'."},
 {"name": "Both: the first of a kind", "who": "Many modern historians of historiography, such as John Marincola", "view": "There were many ways of keeping the past before Herodotus; his was new in combining inquiry, prose, a great war, fairness to the enemy and a search for causes. 'First historian' is true if we mean the first historian of this Greek kind.", "evidence": "The differences between Herodotus and Babylonian chronicles or Israelite history: one inquires, one records, one interprets."}
